{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.Degree.Definitions","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.Degree.Lemmas","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.Div","lean:module:Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.Span","lean:module:Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.Operations","lean:module:Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.Quotient.Operations","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Subring.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":49,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.zHabiroLimit_coord_eq_eulerTrunc","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.zHabiroLimit_coord_eq_eulerTrunc","kind":"theorem","line":1088,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L1088-L1101","doc":"**The brane-tower limit reproduces the Euler truncation tower of `Φ_q`\n(sorry-free).** For each `n` there is a cyclotomic family `c` with the `n`-th\ncoordinate of `zHabiroLimit qinv c` equal to `π_n(Φ̃_q^{(n)})` — the abstract\ninverse-limit element literally carries the quantum dilogarithm's truncation\ndata, level by level. The level-independent coherent `c` is the residual\nclosed (conditionally) by `zHabiroLimit_eq_euler`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.eulerTrunc_eq_cyclotomic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.eulerTrunc_eq_cyclotomic","kind":"theorem","line":1060,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L1060-L1086","doc":"**Per-level `Φ_q` identification (sorry-free).** Under `IsUnit qinv` and\n`Nontrivial R`, the Euler truncation `Φ̃_q^{(n)} = ∑_{m ≤ n} b_m z^m` equals a\nbrane-tower cyclotomic partial sum `∑_{m ≤ n} C(c_m) · P_m`. Hence the genuine\n`Trunc_{<(n+1)}[Φ_q]` is a level of a brane-tower limit. The `c` here depends\non `n` (per-level base change); a level-INDEPENDENT `c` is supplied by the\nq-difference recurrence (`eulerCoeff_unique` + `zHabiroLimit_eq_euler`)\nconditional on the functional equation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.eulerTrunc_degree_lt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.eulerTrunc_degree_lt","kind":"theorem","line":1054,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L1054-L1058","doc":"`eulerTrunc qinv n` has degree `< n + 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.eulerTrunc","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.eulerTrunc","kind":"def","line":1047,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L1047-L1052","doc":"The **Euler truncation tower** `Φ̃_q^{(n)} := ∑_{m ≤ n} b_m z^m`, the genuine\ndegree-`≤ n` monomial truncation of `Φ_q`, with `b_m = eulerCoeff` the Euler\ncoefficients. This is `conj:brane-tower-z-habiro`'s `Trunc_{<n}[Φ_q]` in the\nmonomial basis, over generic `R`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.cyclo_sum_coeff_top","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.cyclo_sum_coeff_top","kind":"theorem","line":1031,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L1031-L1045","doc":"**Top-coefficient extraction (uniqueness core).** For a monic degree-graded\nfamily `g`, the coefficient of `∑_{m ≤ n} C (c m) g m` at degree `n` is\nexactly `c n` — the lower terms have degree `< n` and `g n` is monic. Hence\nthe cyclotomic coefficients are uniquely recoverable (the base change is\ntriangular with unit diagonal, hence injective). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.cyclo_sum_degree_lt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.cyclo_sum_degree_lt","kind":"theorem","line":1014,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L1014-L1029","doc":"A cyclotomic-basis combination has degree `< n`: each summand `C (c m) * g m`\nhas degree `≤ m < n`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.cyclo_repr_exists","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.cyclo_repr_exists","kind":"theorem","line":973,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L973-L1012","doc":"**Triangular base change (existence).** For a monic, degree-graded family\n`g : ℕ → R[X]` (`(g m).degree = m`), every polynomial of degree `< n` is an\n`R`-linear combination of `g 0, …, g (n-1)` (i.e. `{g m}_{m<n}` spans\n`Polynomial.degreeLT R n`). Proved by induction, peeling the top coefficient\nagainst the monic `g (n-1)`. The brane-tower family `braneMonic qinv` is such\na family, so this gives the existence of cyclotomic coordinates. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneMonic_coords_unique","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneMonic_coords_unique","kind":"theorem","line":919,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L919-L963","doc":"**Uniqueness of cyclotomic coordinates (linear independence of the monic\nbasis).** If `∑_{m ≤ n} d_m · g_m = 0` then every `d_m = 0`: the monic\nz-Habiro partial products `g_0, …, g_n` (strictly increasing degrees, each\nmonic) are linearly independent over `R`. Hence the cyclotomic coordinates\nof any polynomial are UNIQUE — the algebraic backbone of \"pinning the\n`c_n`\": at most one coefficient family realises a given truncation tower.\nProved by descending on the top degree: the `X^n`-coefficient of the sum is\n`d_n` (only `g_n` reaches degree `n`, and it is monic), forcing `d_n = 0`,\nthen recursing on the lower sum. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneMonic_coeff_eq_zero_of_lt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneMonic_coeff_eq_zero_of_lt","kind":"theorem","line":912,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L912-L917","doc":"The `n`-th coefficient of `g_m` vanishes when `m < n` (degree `m < n`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneMonic_coeff_self","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneMonic_coeff_self","kind":"theorem","line":903,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L903-L910","doc":"The top coefficient of the monic associate `g_n`: it is `1` (monic of\ndegree `n`). Used for the linear-independence / uniqueness argument. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.zHabiroLimit_eq_euler","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.zHabiroLimit_eq_euler","kind":"theorem","line":881,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L881-L901","doc":"**Conditional closure of item 2(i): the z-Habiro limit is the localised\nEuler element.** GIVEN that a cyclotomic coefficient family `c` satisfies the\nnormalisation `c 0 = 1` and the q-difference recurrence\n`c_{n+1} = c_n · q^{-1} · (1 - q^{-(n+1)})⁻¹` — exactly the coefficient form\nof the analytic functional equation `(1 - z)·Φ_q(qz) = Φ_q(z)` (see\n`QOU.MassEndomorphism.PhiQConvergence.quantumDilog_functional_eq`) together\nwith `Φ_q(0) = 1` — the z-Habiro Cauchy limit `zHabiroLimit qinv c` coincides\nwith `zHabiroLimit qinv (eulerCoeff qinv)`, the specific element built from\nthe localised Euler coefficients.\n\nThis closes the WHICH-FAMILY / base-ring half of `conj:brane-tower-z-habiro`\nitem 2(i), conditional only on the functional equation: any limit whose\ncoefficients obey the q-difference recurrence IS the localised-Euler limit\n(by `eulerCoeff_unique`). The sole remaining input is the analytic functional\nequation itself (the `tprod` reindex in `PhiQConvergence`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.eulerCoeff_unique","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.eulerCoeff_unique","kind":"theorem","line":853,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L853-L879","doc":"**Uniqueness from the first-order q-difference recurrence.** The Euler\ncoefficients are the UNIQUE sequence with `b 0 = 1` obeying the depth\nrecurrence `b_{n+1} = b_n · q^{-1} · (1 - q^{-(n+1)})⁻¹`.\n\nThis is the coefficient form of the analytic-bridge mechanism: `Φ_q` is the\nunique solution of its first-order q-difference equation\n`(1 - z)·Φ_q(qz) = Φ_q(z)` together with the single normalisation\n`Φ_q(0) = 1` — evaluation at the one generic (non-degenerate) point `z = 0`\nplus the functional equation (which relates the two points `z` and `qz`)\npins every coefficient. The coefficient recurrence above is exactly that\nfunctional equation read off `[z^n]` (`b_m = b_{m-1}·q^{m-1}/(q^m-1)`, and\n`q^m-1 = q^m(1-q^{-m})` is a unit times a cyclotomic factor, so the whole\nsequence stays in the cyclotomic localisation). Hence identifying the\nabstract `zHabiroLimit` coefficient family with the actual `Φ_q` reduces to\nchecking these two hypotheses — the genuinely-analytic residue being only\nthat the analytic `Φ_q` satisfies the functional equation\n(`QOU.MassEndomorphism.PhiQConvergence`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.eulerCoeff_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.eulerCoeff_succ","kind":"theorem","line":844,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L844-L851","doc":"Recurrence `b_{n+1} = b_n · q^{-1} · (1 - q^{-(n+1)})⁻¹` — the depth step of\nthe Euler coefficients (the cyclotomic denominator gains one factor). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.eulerCoeff_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.eulerCoeff_zero","kind":"theorem","line":841,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L841-L842","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.eulerCoeff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.eulerCoeff","kind":"def","line":830,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L830-L839","doc":"**The Euler series coefficient** `b_n = [z^n] Φ_q = q^{-n} / ∏_{j=1}^n (1 - q^{-j})`,\nrealised in a base ring `R` where the cyclotomic factors `1 - qinv^j` are\nunits (the cyclotomic localisation of `ℤ[q^±1]`). Written with `qinv = q⁻¹`\nand `Ring.inverse` for the cyclotomic denominators; over the localisation\nthese inverses are genuine. This is the closed Euler form the compute\nwitness `z-habiro-increment-probe` uses (`series_coeff_formula`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.truncSection_zHabiroLimit","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.truncSection_zHabiroLimit","kind":"theorem","line":781,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L781-L798","doc":"**The brane-tower limit, truncated to degree `< n`, is the literal\n`Trunc_{<n}` of the partial sum.** Applying the `Trunc_{<n}` section to the\n`n`-th coordinate of `zHabiroLimit qinv c` returns the genuine degree-`< n`\ntruncation `(Σ_{m ≤ n} c_m · P_m) %ₘ g_n` of the brane-tower partial sum.\n\nThis is the literal \"`Trunc_{<n}` of the brane-tower partial sum\" of\n`conj:brane-tower-z-habiro` §\"Statement\": the abstract inverse-limit\ncoordinate (`zHabiroLimit_coe_apply`) and the concrete degree-`< n`\nrepresentative (`truncSection`) agree. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.degree_truncSection_lt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.degree_truncSection_lt","kind":"theorem","line":755,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L755-L769","doc":"**The section lands in degrees `< n`.** `(truncSection … x).degree < n`:\nthe chosen representative `p %ₘ g_n` has degree strictly below\n`deg g_n = n` (`degree_modByMonic_lt`, `g_n` monic of degree `n`). This is\nthe defining property of the canonical degree-`< n` representative — the\nliteral `Trunc_{<n}` of `def:z-habiro-ring`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneMonic_degree","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneMonic_degree","kind":"theorem","line":743,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L743-L753","doc":"The degree of the monic associate `g_n` is exactly `n`\n(`Monic.degree_mul` + `degree_X_sub_C`). `Nontrivial R` ensures each linear\nfactor `X - C(q^k)` has degree `1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.mk_truncSection","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.mk_truncSection","kind":"theorem","line":723,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L723-L741","doc":"**`truncSection` is a genuine section of `Ideal.Quotient.mk`.**\n`mk ∘ truncSection = id`: the chosen degree-`< n` representative\n`p %ₘ g_n` lands in the same class as `p`, because\n`g_n ∣ (p %ₘ g_n - p)` (`modByMonic_add_div`). So `truncSection` picks a\nrepresentative in each fibre of the projection. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.truncSection_mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.truncSection_mk","kind":"theorem","line":718,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L718-L721","doc":"The section on a class `mk p` is the canonical remainder `p %ₘ g_n`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.truncSection","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.truncSection","kind":"def","line":698,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L698-L716","doc":"**The `Trunc_{<n}` section** `R[X] ⧸ (P_n) → R[X]`: the canonical\ndegree-`< n` representative of a class, given by division-with-remainder\n`p ↦ p %ₘ g_n` against the monic associate `g_n = braneMonic qinv n`\n(`Polynomial.modByMonic`). Well-defined by `modByMonic_well_defined`.\n\nThis discharges file-header \"What remains OPEN\" item 3 — the canonical\ndegree-`< n` section identifying `R[X]/(P_n)` with the truncations\n`Trunc_{<n}[Φ_q]` that the conjecture's `Φ̃^(N)` uses. Hypothesis:\n`IsUnit qinv` (so `P_n` has a unit leading coefficient, hence a monic\nassociate). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.modByMonic_well_defined","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.modByMonic_well_defined","kind":"theorem","line":683,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L683-L696","doc":"**Well-definedness of the degree-`< n` representative.** If\n`p ≡ p' (mod braneIdeal qinv n)` then `p %ₘ g_n = p' %ₘ g_n`: the ideal is\n`(g_n)` (monic associate), so `g_n ∣ (p - p')`, hence\n`(p - p') %ₘ g_n = 0` (`modByMonic_eq_zero_iff_dvd`); `sub_modByMonic`\ndistributes `%ₘ` over the difference. This is the descent condition that\nmakes `truncSection` well-defined on the quotient. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneIdeal_eq_span_braneMonic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneIdeal_eq_span_braneMonic","kind":"theorem","line":672,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L672-L681","doc":"**The z-Habiro filtration ideal is generated by the monic associate.**\n`braneIdeal qinv n = (g_n)` for `g_n = braneMonic qinv n`, because the\ngenerator `branePoly qinv n` differs from `g_n` by the unit `C (u_n)`\n(`Ideal.span_singleton_mul_left_unit`). So the modulus for the\ndegree-`< n` section may be taken to be the *monic* `g_n` while the ideal\nof the completion chain (`braneIdeal`, `def:z-habiro-ring`) is unchanged. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.branePoly_eq_unit_mul_braneMonic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.branePoly_eq_unit_mul_braneMonic","kind":"theorem","line":651,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L651-L670","doc":"**`branePoly` is a unit multiple of its monic associate.** Under\n`IsUnit qinv`, `branePoly qinv n = C (u_n) * braneMonic qinv n` with\n`u_n := braneUnit qinv n` a unit, because each factor factors as\n`1 - C(qinv^{k}) · X = C(-(qinv^{k})) · (X - C((qinv^{k})⁻¹))`\n(using `Ring.mul_inverse_cancel` since `qinv^k` is a unit). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneUnit_isUnit","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneUnit_isUnit","kind":"theorem","line":644,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L644-L649","doc":"`u_n` is a unit whenever `qinv` is — a product of units `-(qinv^k)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneUnit","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneUnit","kind":"def","line":636,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L636-L642","doc":"The unit by which `branePoly` and its monic associate `braneMonic` differ:\n`u_n := ∏_{k=1}^n (-(qinv^k))`. Under `IsUnit qinv` it is a unit\n(`braneUnit_isUnit`); it is the leading coefficient of `branePoly qinv n`,\nnamely `(-1)^n qinv^{n(n+1)/2}`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneMonic_monic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneMonic_monic","kind":"theorem","line":630,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L630-L634","doc":"`g_n` is monic — a product of monic linear factors `X - C(q^k)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneMonic_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneMonic_succ","kind":"theorem","line":626,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L626-L628","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneMonic_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneMonic_zero","kind":"theorem","line":624,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L624-L624","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneMonic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneMonic","kind":"def","line":613,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L613-L622","doc":"The **monic** z-Habiro partial product\n`g_n := ∏_{k=1}^n (X - C(Ring.inverse (qinv^k)))`. Each factor is monic\n(`monic_X_sub_C`), so the product is monic of degree `n`\n(`braneMonic_monic`, `braneMonic_degree`). Under `IsUnit qinv` this is the\ncanonical monic associate of `branePoly qinv n`\n(`branePoly_eq_unit_mul_braneMonic`), used as the modulus for the\ndegree-`< n` division-with-remainder section of `Trunc_{<n}`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.zHabiroLimit_coe_apply","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.zHabiroLimit_coe_apply","kind":"theorem","line":581,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L581-L589","doc":"The `n`-th component of the brane-tower limit element is the cyclotomic\nprojection `π_n(Σ_{m ≤ n} c_m · P_m)` of the depth-`n` partial sum — the\n\"value of the limit at level `n`\". A projection-compatibility lemma making\nthe limit element's coordinates explicit. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.zHabiroLimit","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.zHabiroLimit","kind":"def","line":556,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L556-L579","doc":"**The brane-tower cyclotomic Cauchy limit, AS AN ELEMENT of the z-Habiro\ncompletion ring.**\n\nFor any coefficient family `c : ℕ → R`, the cyclotomic partial-sum tower\n`a n = Σ_{m ≤ n} c_m · P_m` has, by `cyclotomicPartialSum_isCauchy`, a\ncoherent inverse-limit thread `n ↦ π_n(a n)`. This packages that thread as\na genuine element of `ZHabiroCompletion qinv` — the algebraic Cauchy LIMIT\nof the brane-tower truncation tower, living *inside* the completion ring,\nUNCONDITIONALLY and over generic `[CommRing R]` (§7c).\n\nThe compatibility witness (the `Subring` membership proof) is exactly\n`quantumDilog_zHabiro_cauchy_limit_cyclotomic qinv c`. This is the\n\"limit exists as an algebraic element\" content of `conj:brane-tower-z-habiro`\n/ `def:z-habiro-ring`, compute-backed by\n`computations/z-habiro-increment-probe.witness.json` (the `Φ_q` truncation\ntower IS a cyclotomic partial sum). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.mem_ZHabiroCompletion","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.mem_ZHabiroCompletion","kind":"theorem","line":542,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L542-L546","doc":"Membership in the completion ring is *exactly* thread-compatibility. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.instCommRingSubtypeForallQuotientPolynomialIdealBraneIdealMemSubringZHabiroCompletion","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.instCommRingSubtypeForallQuotientPolynomialIdealBraneIdealMemSubringZHabiroCompletion","kind":"instance","line":536,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L536-L540","doc":"The z-Habiro completion ring is commutative — it is a subring of the\ncommutative product ring `Π n, R[X] ⧸ I_n`. (Recorded explicitly so\ndownstream code can `inferInstance` a `CommRing (ZHabiroCompletion qinv)`.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.ZHabiroCompletion","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.ZHabiroCompletion","kind":"def","line":505,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L505-L534","doc":"**The z-Habiro completion ring** `varprojlim_n R[X]/(P_n)`, realised as the\nsubring of *compatible threads* in the product `Π n, R[X] ⧸ braneIdeal qinv n`.\n\nA family `x : Π n, R[X] ⧸ I_n` is a thread of the inverse system iff every\nbonding map `factor_{n+1→n} : R[X]/I_{n+1} → R[X]/I_n` carries `x (n+1)` to\n`x n`. The carrier is that compatibility set; closure under the ring\noperations is immediate because each `Ideal.Quotient.factor (…)` is a\n`RingHom` (`map_mul`, `map_add`, `map_one`, `map_zero`, `map_neg`).\n\n`Subring.toCommRing` (the product is commutative, so the subring is too)\nsupplies the `CommRing` instance — the z-Habiro completion is a `CommRing`.\nThis is the inverse-limit object of `def:z-habiro-ring`'s cyclotomic\ncompletion `varprojlim_n ℤ[q^±1][z]/(P_n)`, modelled over generic\n`[CommRing R]` (§7c). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.quantumDilog_zHabiro_cauchy_limit_cyclotomic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.quantumDilog_zHabiro_cauchy_limit_cyclotomic","kind":"theorem","line":462,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L462-L486","doc":"**Algebraic Cauchy-limit thread (z-Habiro) — UNCONDITIONAL for the\nbrane-tower shape.** Composing the shape lemma\n`cyclotomicPartialSum_isCauchy` with the conditional limit-thread\n`quantumDilog_zHabiro_cauchy_limit`: for *every* coefficient family\n`c : ℕ → R`, the cyclotomic partial-sum sequence\n`a n = Σ_{m ≤ n} c_m · P_m` has a coherent inverse-limit thread\n`n ↦ π_n(a n)` — compatible with every bonding map of\n`varprojlim_n R[X]/(P_n)`. No `IsCauchyFiltration` hypothesis is required:\nit is supplied by the shape lemma, which the compute witness\n`z-habiro-increment-probe` backs (the `Φ_q` truncation tower IS a\ncyclotomic partial sum). This discharges file-header \"What remains OPEN\"\nitem 1 for the cyclotomic shape: the increment hypothesis is now a\ntheorem. Items 2 (`varprojlim` as a `CommRing`) and 3 (`Trunc_{<n}`\nsection) remain open. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.cyclotomicPartialSum_isCauchy","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.cyclotomicPartialSum_isCauchy","kind":"theorem","line":434,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L434-L460","doc":"**Shape lemma — UNCONDITIONAL.** Every cyclotomic partial-sum sequence\n`a n = Σ_{m ≤ n} c_m · P_m` (the brane-tower shape, with coefficient family\n`c : ℕ → R`) satisfies the z-Habiro Cauchy criterion `IsCauchyFiltration`.\n\nThe increment `a (n+1) - a n` telescopes (`Finset.sum_range_succ`) to the\nsingle new term `C (c (n+1)) * branePoly qinv (n+1)`, which lies in\n`I_n = (P_n)` because `P_n ∣ P_{n+1}` (`branePoly_dvd_succ`). This is the\nLean realisation of the compute witness\n`computations/z-habiro-increment-probe.witness.json`\n(`increment_checks[*].divisible_by_P_n = true`, `remainder = 0`): the\nquantum-dilog truncation tower *is* a cyclotomic partial sum, so its\nincrement-coherence hypothesis is a theorem, not a conjecture. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.quantumDilog_zHabiro_cauchy_limit","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.quantumDilog_zHabiro_cauchy_limit","kind":"theorem","line":384,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L384-L415","doc":"**Algebraic Cauchy-limit thread (z-Habiro) — sorry-free, conditional.**\n\nFor a sequence `(a_n)` Cauchy in the z-Habiro filtration\n(`IsCauchyFiltration`), the coherent family of projections\n`n ↦ π_n^{(z)}(a_n)` is compatible with every bonding map of the inverse\nsystem: the bonding map `factor_{n+1→n}` sends `π_{n+1}(a_{n+1})` to\n`π_n(a_n)`. Hence the thread `(π_n(a_n))_n` is a genuine element of\n`varprojlim_n R[X]/(P_n)` — the algebraic limit of `(a_n)` in\n`def:z-habiro-ring`. Proved from `Ideal.Quotient.factor_mk` (the bonding\nmap is `mk`-functorial) and the increment criterion `hcauchy`.\n\nNOTE: this is the *conjectural-Cauchy-limit half* of\n`conj:brane-tower-z-habiro`, discharged modulo the increment hypothesis;\nthe analytic prerequisite is closed in\n`QOU.MassEndomorphism.PhiQConvergence.quantumDilog_multipliable`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.cauchy_proj_stabilises","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.cauchy_proj_stabilises","kind":"theorem","line":355,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L355-L364","doc":"The cyclotomic projections of a Cauchy sequence agree on the tail,\nphrased as a congruence in the quotient `R[X] / I_n`. This is\n`cauchy_tail_mem` repackaged via `Ideal.Quotient.eq` — the form in\nwhich the inverse-limit thread `(π_n(a_n))_n` is read off. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.cauchy_tail_mem","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.cauchy_tail_mem","kind":"theorem","line":322,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L322-L353","doc":"**Increment-coherence Cauchy core.** If `(a_n)` satisfies the\nincrement criterion, then for every `m ≥ n` the difference\n`a_m - a_n ∈ I_n`.\n\nEquivalently: the cyclotomic projections\n`π_n^{(z)}(a_m) = π_n^{(z)}(a_n)` agree for all `m ≥ n`, so the thread\n`(π_n^{(z)}(a_n))_n` is a coherent element of the inverse system\n`varprojlim_n R[X]/(P_n)` — i.e. `(a_n)` converges in the z-Habiro\nfiltration topology. This is the precise algebraic Cauchy content of\n`conj:brane-tower-z-habiro` §\"Statement\", discharged from the\ndescending chain `braneIdeal_le_of_le`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.IsCauchyFiltration","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.IsCauchyFiltration","kind":"def","line":314,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L314-L320","doc":"A sequence `a : ℕ → R[X]` is **Cauchy for the z-Habiro filtration**\niff each increment lands in the corresponding ideal:\n`a_{n+1} - a_n ∈ I_n`. In the filtration (linear) topology whose\nneighbourhood basis of `0` is `{I_n}`, this is exactly the statement\nthat `(a_n)` is a Cauchy sequence — the increments tend to `0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneIdeal_le_of_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneIdeal_le_of_le","kind":"theorem","line":306,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L306-L310","doc":"**Descending chain (general):** `I_m ≤ I_n` for `n ≤ m`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneIdeal_antitone","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneIdeal_antitone","kind":"theorem","line":296,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L296-L304","doc":"**Descending chain (single step):** `I_{n+1} ≤ I_n`.\nThis is the defining property of the z-Habiro filtration —\n`(P_{n+1}) ⊆ (P_n)` because `P_n ∣ P_{n+1}`. It makes the cyclotomic\ninverse limit `varprojlim_n R[X]/(P_n)` well-defined (the bonding maps\n`R[X]/(P_{n+1}) → R[X]/(P_n)` exist). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneIdeal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.braneIdeal","kind":"def","line":290,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L290-L294","doc":"The z-Habiro filtration ideal `I_n = (P_n(q,z))` of\n`ℤ[q^±1][z] = R[X]`. The cyclotomic completion of `def:z-habiro-ring`\nis the inverse limit of `R[X] / I_n` along this descending chain. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.branePoly_dvd_of_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.branePoly_dvd_of_le","kind":"theorem","line":276,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L276-L286","doc":"The divisibility chain is monotone: `P_n ∣ P_m` whenever `n ≤ m`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.branePoly_dvd_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.branePoly_dvd_succ","kind":"theorem","line":270,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L270-L274","doc":"Each partial product divides the next: `P_n ∣ P_{n+1}`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.branePoly_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.branePoly_succ","kind":"theorem","line":267,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L267-L268","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.branePoly_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.branePoly_zero","kind":"theorem","line":265,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L265-L265","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.branePoly","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassEndomorphism.ZHabiroCauchy.branePoly","kind":"def","line":258,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassEndomorphism/ZHabiroCauchy.lean#L258-L263","doc":"The `n`-th z-Habiro partial product\n`P_n(q,z) = ∏_{k=1}^{n} (1 - q^{-k} z)`, written with `qinv = q⁻¹`,\n`z = X`. `P 0 = 1`; `P (n+1) = P n · (1 - qinv^{n+1} · X)`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}